---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import OsIcon from '../components/OsIcon.astro';
import Limits from '../components/Limits.astro';
import { RELEASE, REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="Free, private email client for Windows 10 and 11 · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren is a free, open-source email client for Windows 10 and 11. Gmail and IMAP in one inbox, mail encrypted on your PC, search in milliseconds. 14 MB."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>A free, private email client for Windows</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      OpenWren is an open-source email client for Windows 10 and 11. It puts Gmail and your other accounts in one
      inbox, keeps your mail on your own PC in an encrypted database, and finds any message in milliseconds. No
      account to create, no ads, no subscription.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={RELEASE.windows}><OsIcon os="windows" /> Download {RELEASE.version} for Windows</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/download/#windows">Install steps</a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">For 64-bit Windows 10 and 11. A 14 MB download.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <Screenshot src="inbox.png" alt="The OpenWren inbox: folders on the left, the message list in the middle and a message on the right" label="Inbox" width={760} />

    <h2>Why people look for another Windows mail program</h2>
    <p>
      Windows Mail is gone, and the new Outlook that replaced it shows ads in its free version and, for accounts that
      are not Microsoft's, keeps a copy of your mail on Microsoft's servers. Many people simply want what Windows Mail
      was: a small program that shows their mail, with a search that works.
    </p>
    <p>OpenWren is that, with a few things added:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search that keeps up.</strong> One search box for all accounts, about 30 ms on a mailbox of 200,000 messages. <a href="/search/">How search works</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Your mail stays on your PC.</strong> It is stored in an encrypted database, and the app talks only to your mail servers. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> lists every network request.</li>
      <li><strong>Trackers removed.</strong> Tracking pixels and tracking codes in links are taken out, and remote images stay blocked until you ask for them.</li>
      <li><strong>Warnings on suspicious mail</strong>, for example when a sender's name claims to be a company the address does not belong to.</li>
      <li><strong>Small.</strong> The window uses the web view that is already part of Windows, so the download is 14 MB.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Installing on Windows</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Download the installer and run it.</li>
      <li>
        Windows may show "Windows protected your PC". The installer is not signed with a paid certificate yet, so
        Windows does not know it. Choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>Follow the installer. It installs for your user only and needs no administrator rights.</li>
      <li>Add your account: your address and password for IMAP, or your Gmail address and an <a href="/docs/#gmail-with-an-app-password">app password</a>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      You do not have to take the file on trust: its checksum is published in
      <a href={RELEASE.checksums}>SHA256SUMS</a>, and the whole source code is in the
      <a href={REPO_URL}>repository</a>. OpenWren tells you when a new version is out and installs it for you.
    </p>
    <p>Passwords are kept in Windows Credential Manager, not in OpenWren's files.</p>

    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <Limits />
    <p>
      If your mail is a Microsoft 365 work account, Outlook is still the program for you. See
      <a href="/outlook-alternative/">OpenWren as an Outlook alternative</a> for an honest comparison.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <h3>Is it really free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. OpenWren is free software under the Apache 2.0 licence. There is no paid version.</p>
    <h3>Does it work with Gmail?</h3>
    <p>Yes, with an app password from your Google account. Gmail labels are shown as folders.</p>
    <h3>Does it work with Outlook.com or Hotmail addresses?</h3>
    <p>Not yet. Microsoft accounts need a sign-in method that OpenWren does not have yet.</p>
    <h3>Can I remove it completely?</h3>
    <p>Yes. The <a href="/download/#windows">download page</a> lists what to delete, including the stored mail and passwords.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
